1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:01,950
An Android phone left unused for years

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,690
used to fetch just a few dozen yuan from recyclers

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,560
But this March, something strange happened

4
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:09,020
A recycling stall owner said

5
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:11,860
these phones now go for over 400

6
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:13,700
even with a cracked screen

7
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:16,020
Two lines were trending for days

8
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:18,140
Used phones once worth just 10 yuan

9
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:19,770
now fetch 500 from recyclers

10
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:22,040
Three old phones can get you an iPhone

11
00:00:22,090 --> 00:00:23,760
Seeing news like this

12
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:26,800
most people's first thought: recyclers are hyping it

13
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:29,670
How could electronics gain value just sitting there

14
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,030
Stranger still

15
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,520
after rising for half a year, starting March 27

16
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:35,840
recycling prices fell 30% in five days

17
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:37,740
and some models were cut in half

18
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:41,200
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics, number 9

19
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:42,360
the law of supply

20
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:43,960
Last time I left a question

21
00:00:44,010 --> 00:00:47,380
Prices rose and fewer people bought, but what about sellers

22
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:51,310
Calling it hype isn't unfair

23
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:54,700
CCTV reporters asked two big platforms in Beijing, and staff said

24
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:57,740
old phones hadn't gone up much, the rumors were just hype

25
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:58,820
Experts also said

26
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:01,840
that "three for an iPhone" talk was an emotional bubble

27
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:04,920
But in the same report, several recycling stalls said

28
00:01:04,970 --> 00:01:07,490
some Android phones really were up 5 to 6 times

29
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:11,070
A phone repair shop owner in Hangzhou did the math best

30
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:15,750
an OPPO A93 256G went from just over 100 to 300

31
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:19,410
One 256G memory chip alone sells for 300 to 400

32
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:20,850
Cracked screens don't matter

33
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:24,330
Basic keypad phones have no memory chip, and their price didn't budge

34
00:01:24,570 --> 00:01:28,070
It's not the phone that went up, it's the memory chip inside

35
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:29,380
Why are chips so valuable

36
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:32,670
I covered that in my video on why memory suddenly got pricey

37
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:35,250
AI took over memory makers' capacity

38
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:36,890
so regular memory ran short

39
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:39,960
so short that even chips in old phones are wanted

40
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,780
They get pulled out and put into cheap electronics

41
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:44,470
sold in Southeast Asia and Africa

42
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:46,600
Let me translate that for you

43
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:51,060
The higher the price, the more people are willing and able to sell

44
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:53,440
Last episode ended asking: what about sellers

45
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:54,950
This time, the seller is you

46
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:57,530
and that old phone of yours

47
00:01:57,580 --> 00:02:00,370
So how many old phones have piled up in your drawer

48
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,970
China retires 600 to 700 million phones a year

49
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,390
about one for every two people

50
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:09,230
Stats say once phones are retired, over half stay at home

51
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,550
only about 5% go to formal recycling

52
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:13,880
Once the price is called out, drawers open

53
00:02:13,930 --> 00:02:16,260
One recycling platform, Shuma Huishou Wang

54
00:02:16,310 --> 00:02:19,249
went from handling 200,000 phones a day last September

55
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,970
to 1.5 million a day this March

56
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:23,400
a jump of over 7 times

57
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,120
Collectors rushed in too

58
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:26,760
One veteran recycler estimates

59
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:29,470
the trade had about 100,000 people last year

60
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,110
and over a million this year

61
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:32,480
Someone joked online

62
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:35,310
barbers are buying, fruit sellers are buying

63
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:38,440
Once the price is called out, sellers and buyers flood in

64
00:02:38,490 --> 00:02:39,830
But chips are still scarce

65
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:43,610
so why did old phone prices collapse in just five days

66
00:02:44,530 --> 00:02:46,360
Let me bring in a concept

67
00:02:46,410 --> 00:02:48,590
What exactly is elasticity of supply

68
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:52,390
When price moves, how much can supply move, and how fast

69
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:53,900
Memory comes from two places

70
00:02:53,950 --> 00:02:55,380
One is the factory

71
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:57,580
the other is your drawer

72
00:02:57,760 --> 00:02:59,990
The drawer supply already exists

73
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,840
the phones were made long ago, they're just old

74
00:03:03,030 --> 00:03:05,010
All it takes is a price to call them out

75
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,990
so it can multiply in just a few months

76
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:10,220
Last time I said buyers can be rubber bands or steel wire

77
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:11,640
Sellers are the same

78
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:15,300
The drawer is a soft rubber band, it stretches at a tug

79
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:17,110
The factory is the steel wire

80
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,810
SK hynix said on its October earnings call

81
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:23,499
that through 2029, its DRAM and HBM capacity

82
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:25,010
is almost sold out

83
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:27,200
New fabs take a long time to build

84
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:29,540
and the crunch will last into 2030

85
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:32,130
Even new fabs get the shell built first

86
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:35,010
with clean rooms added in phases, for fear of a glut

87
00:03:35,070 --> 00:03:37,810
The way I see it, when the same memory chip gets pricier

88
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:40,150
the first to answer is always your drawer

89
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:43,700
By the time factories catch up, years have passed

90
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:46,640
But salvaged chips have only a few buyers

91
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:48,480
factories that make cheap electronics

92
00:03:48,530 --> 00:03:51,750
Reports say almost no brand will use second-hand chips

93
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:53,430
A chip recycler said

94
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,760
a factory can use tens of thousands a day

95
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:57,880
but over a million flow in

96
00:03:57,950 --> 00:03:59,910
So starting March 27

97
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:01,500
down over 30% in five days

98
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:04,030
the Honor X9 dropped from 320 to 130

99
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:07,540
On April 1, many Huaqiangbei recyclers stopped buying

100
00:04:07,590 --> 00:04:10,880
One recycler has a warehouse of phones he paid over 5 million for

101
00:04:10,930 --> 00:04:13,990
He sealed it up, since selling would lose him over 1 million

102
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:17,360
The faster a rubber band stretches, the faster it snaps back

103
00:04:17,410 --> 00:04:19,800
When prices fall, the buyers stop first

104
00:04:19,890 --> 00:04:21,430
and the drawers close again

105
00:04:21,590 --> 00:04:25,020
Price can open drawers, but it can't conjure a new factory

106
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,520
I'll state the limits just once

107
00:04:27,570 --> 00:04:29,620
Not every old phone went up

108
00:04:29,730 --> 00:04:30,990
Apple phones barely moved

109
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,870
an 8-year-old iPhone 6s is worth only about 50 yuan

110
00:04:33,930 --> 00:04:35,250
Basic phones stayed flat too

111
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:39,170
Platforms say budget and mid-range Androids rose about 10% on average

112
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:42,060
the big gainers were scrapped phones with lots of storage

113
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:46,290
Trending claims like "50 times" or "three for an iPhone"

114
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:49,920
were called extreme by CCTV's investigation

115
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:52,770
So next time something you own suddenly gains value

116
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:54,130
ask one question first

117
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:56,900
How many people have the same thing as me

118
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:59,240
and how fast can they bring it out

119
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:01,120
If every home has one in a drawer

120
00:05:01,170 --> 00:05:04,610
high prices won't last, so don't wait for the peak to sell

121
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:07,670
If more needs new factories, prices stay high for long

122
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:09,300
Memory sticks are like that

123
00:05:09,510 --> 00:05:11,040
Back to that trending post

124
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:13,010
"Three phones can get you an iPhone"

125
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:15,130
My take: it won't hold

126
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:16,720
Hype is only part of it

127
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:19,050
The bigger reason is, three old phones

128
00:05:19,100 --> 00:05:20,620
are in every household's drawer

129
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:24,310
While writing this episode, I went through my own drawers

130
00:05:24,390 --> 00:05:25,950
and found a few old phones

131
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,370
but when I checked, their real prices hadn't gone up

132
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:32,510
So how many used phones are in your drawer

133
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:34,720
At a used phone shop in Huaqiangbei

134
00:05:34,770 --> 00:05:36,680
someone paid 1399

135
00:05:36,730 --> 00:05:39,170
for a model that cost nearly 3000 new

136
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:41,310
That "I got a deal" feeling a buyer has

137
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:43,060
economics has a name for that too

138
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:46,440
Next time, we'll look at principle number 10

139
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:47,860
consumer surplus
